{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob08re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob08re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n4le02ob08re2img01.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><span class=\"strong\"> John</span>: Today I'm going to tell you about Composite structure diagrams. They're very useful when you've got to control run-time instances that collaborate by using communication links. Before we start talking about it, I will tell you that you haven't got to keep your questions to yourselves, you can ask any questions if you need to. So, let's get started! This kind of diagram works by enabling collaboration, which shows cooperation between different instances\u2026<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sally</span>: Excuse me, sir. Are these similar to collaboration diagrams? <br/>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n4le02ob08re2img02.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><span class=\"strong\"> John</span>: They share some elements, but Collaboration diagrams are what we call Communication diagrams in UML 2. In fact, CS diagrams can show boxes containing a classifier which shows steps to follow to accomplish something. For example, the different stages to enroll in a course, such as the requirements, selection tests, form completion, etc. Any questions? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sally</span>: No, I haven't got any. However, what you're describing sounds very similar to the structure of an object role model diagram. <br/>", "time": "35500"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n4le02ob08re2img03.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><span class=\"strong\"> John</span>: You're right Sally! But you haven't got to confuse them, it's important to note that this similarity mostly relates to their notation. Additionally, these two kinds of diagrams deal with issues and structures differently. For example, when you use ORM diagrams they will focus more on relationships between entities. On the other hand, CS diagrams are centered towards collaborations. Any other comments? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sally</span>: No. I guess I'll ask you for more guidance before I start using this diagram. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> John</span>: Great! Another element to take into account is the fact that editors prefer to use other diagrams to explore collaboration among elements. They say that CS diagrams haven't got a detailed notation. They say that the most appropriate kind of diagram to use in this case is sequence diagrams, especially because it's more widespread among developers. However, after you use this diagram you'll tell me whether you liked it or not. <br/>", "time": "76000"}]}}], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can explain how the composite structure diagram works. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can give details about the composite structure diagram. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify key features related to composite structure diagrams.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Are you familiar with the changes between UML 1.x and 2.x? Are they relevant? Explain.", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "How can you avoid confusions between the different kinds of diagrams?  ", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct